#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091893 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1316" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n2. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n3. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n$(5x^{2} - 10x^{3}) \\div (5x)|= \\frac{5x^{2} - 10x^{3}}{5x}; = (5x(x - 2x^{2}))/(5x); = (5x(x - 2x^{2}))/(5x); = x - 2x^{2}$\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n$\\frac{1 - x^{3}}{x^{3}}|= \\frac{1}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}}; = \\frac{1}{x^{3}} - 1$\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091894 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1317" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3$, the leading term contains an $x^3$.\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2$, the leading term contains an $x^2$.\n\nIn $P-Q$, the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3$. There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P$.\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1318" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n2. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n3. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5$. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10}$, meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10$.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5$. Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0$, which is not a polynomial of degree $5$.\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5$. Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1$, which is a polynomial of degree $0$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9981" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2$, the leading term contains an $x^2$.\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8$, the leading term contains an $x^8$.\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q$, the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing\n\n$$\nx^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b97dfe-e863-5067-bd1f-9fe80a7da4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089431} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe063b-3ac8-5bae-886e-2ef8e28207c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089432} {:answer/id #uuid "16f477bf-e52c-5659-9a0d-6c656f1a72d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089433} {:answer/id #uuid "1aea45fa-1601-5ba3-9238-4fec5c076a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089434} {:answer/id #uuid "42142bd5-3fdf-567d-87bd-f25ee43bf67f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aea45fa-1601-5ba3-9238-4fec5c076a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089434} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc0adf4a-ebc6-5645-94d9-fd6ba43dae74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089430}] :question/id #uuid "c2c512bf-41d1-55e1-a589-f849ec8fac50" :question/math-academy-id "q-141203" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\n1. $(x^{2} + 4x) - (-5x^{3} + 3)$\n2. $(2 - x^{3}) \\div (x^{2})$\n3. $(2x - x^{3}) \\cdot (x^{2} - x + 1)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d11ab003-2440-5e69-9cf5-88cfaddcad5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090432} {:answer/id #uuid "36ae66df-0ea6-5421-8b72-67a89f84f5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090433} {:answer/id #uuid "4496fb53-4831-5dc2-978e-02266897190a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090434} {:answer/id #uuid "46f5c046-8295-5986-8048-2915d9ba8ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090435} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4791ba-530d-5bec-8525-ba6572add1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d11ab003-2440-5e69-9cf5-88cfaddcad5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090432} :answer-field/id #uuid "6439cbca-2e75-581a-916c-bd48c79e58fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090431}] :question/id #uuid "5f528577-792d-5dde-937b-39993322cd56" :question/math-academy-id "q-46457" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $6$, respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P + Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $4$\n2. $P - Q$ is always a polynomial\n3. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7ba49c-4a26-54e2-bd75-42ad05f3b4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294763} {:answer/id #uuid "04abffd8-7053-53e6-b817-84082d046c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294764} {:answer/id #uuid "5a36e3d7-210d-5b4f-a85a-040387b79fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294765} {:answer/id #uuid "9075ff78-741a-5729-812d-2a80fd97eb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294766} {:answer/id #uuid "430bfad4-077d-5435-8e5c-52a43682396e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9075ff78-741a-5729-812d-2a80fd97eb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294766} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a971d13-8c2e-5ca1-b66c-f3ad09971c24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c510ba2-7634-5ceb-8cb3-a5b35044f36d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47447" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P+Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{3}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{7}.$ Then $P-Q={x}^{3}-{x}^{7},$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{7}={x}^{3+7}={x}^{10},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}]}
